- The exponent gates, substituted as scalars, read `(·)^(z²−u^36) = (·)^0 = 1` — information destroyed. As gates, they constrain and the base survives.

Theorem (evaluation order). The HNAN bypass — divisor vanishes → gain exactly 1, no phase write — is not an engineering patch. It is the correct evaluation order of the constraint logic: a gate whose divisor vanishes is asserting a deeper closure, and multiplying by 1 is what "the constraint holds with no residue" means operationally. Scalar substitution is a different, inconsistent theory that happens to agree with the gate logic only on the open set where no gate fires.

