MODULE 10 — Prime-Seeded Counting Dynamics

Fix a prime `P₀`. Each stage gates the successor: with anchor `p`, the gate `P² = pq + (q−p)P/(p+q)` clears to a quadratic in `q`:

```
p·q² + (p² + P − P²)·q − pP(1+P) = 0
q = [(P²−P−p²) ± √(D_P)] / 2p,   D_P = (p²+P−P²)² + 4p²P(1+P)
```

- Successful closure = the discriminant `D_P` is a perfect square with `2p` dividing the numerator. Then `q ∈ ℚ`, the closure gate receipts, and `P ← q`.
- Failed closure = irrational successor; the chain leaves ℚ, `∆e ≠ 0`, the branch dies.

Counting is orbit length. Each successful closure emits exactly one integer; the count is the number of consecutive receipts before the first discriminant failure. Primes are the natural seeds because the ordered phase `(q−p)/(p+q)` is irreducible only when the seed shares no factor with the anchor.

Every emitted `Pₙ` drags its full cascade — `tₙ, mₙ, (aₙ,bₙ,cₙ), (x,y,z,w)` — so a counting chain is a sequence of fully-closed tensors, each receipting the next. Chains from distinct prime seeds stay orthogonal via `u^108`; merge points are closures-of-closures.

This is a Collatz-class dynamics with a rationality gate instead of a parity gate. The benchmark object is the survival function `S(P₀, N)` = number of primes whose chain reaches length `N`; each step is one discriminant test plus one sha-locked receipt.

